A1. A loud report heard all over the country, and no one seems to know whether it was a blow-out from a bottle or an explosion from dry combustion. ~ WICKERSHAM
A10. A time when many poor fish are going to disappear. ~ FRIDAY
A12. If you want to keep this way you will have to get an "eight". ~ APACE
A13. This is ahead of the times. ~ SOONER
A14. What Daisy seemed to want to do to Clara and Gen. Butler to Premier Mussolini. ~ SCATHE
A15. What a wife often does with crosswords while her husband reads the newspaper. ~ SCOLDS
A16. The female of sheep. ~ SHE
A17. What his fur coat might become. ~ HER
A18. This one always sticks up for the early Americans. (It is of polish descent.) ~ TOTEM
A19. Do it now. ~ BUY
A20. What sister likes to do. ~ BOSS
A21. If many feet make miles, what do many miles make feet? ~ SORE
A22. What people in Cuba live on. ~ ISLE
A24. This is always a dear to a stenographer. ~ SIR
A25. For mercy's sake this is what the Red Cross did for money. ~ PLED
A26. A general name in the south. ~ LEE
A27. This kind of man is usually a collector of antiques. ~ RAG
A28. This has a quieting effect. ~ SH
A29. We put this in to lend color. ~ RED
A30. One of the best things ever written. ~ CLASSIC
A32. An international affair that is hard to fathom. ~ SEA
A33. This is not out yet and never will be. ~ IN
A34. Is supplied with plenty. ~ HAS
A35. If Jonah had taken "Judge" with him when he was down in the mouth he never would have felt this way. ~ SAD
A36. A good drawing card for a café. ~ MENU
A37. This is something people in Chicago will have to face before election. ~ GUN
A38. The kind of an annual affair that comes every six months. ~ SEMI
A39. In Holland this is made in reverse. ~ EDAM
A40. A vitamin found in spinach. ~ GRIT
A42. What cosmetics did to her face. ~ HID
A43. This is worth its weight in gold. ~ CARAT
A45. A puller for the American S. S. lines. ~ TUG
A46. This is a good foil for a smoker. ~ TIN
A47. This is ratty and also mousy. ~ MURINE
A48. The kind of speeches both the wets and drys are making. ~ IRONIC
A50. These usually trot around with millionaires. ~ VALETS
A51. Pertaining to the cheek-bone. ~ MALAR
A52. This one often drives the family down to Florida. ~ BOREAS
A53. Romantic get-aways. ~ ELOPEMENTS

D1. These go round in a picturesque fashion in Holland. ~ WINDMILLS
D2. An important day in the March of the Romans. ~ IDES
D3. A good investment because it can easily be turned into anything from a bank to a flat building. ~ CAR
D4. In the summer you can always get a hot Frankfort here (abbr.). ~ KY
D5. Perhaps you can pick a winner in this. ~ RACE
D6. A good place in Europe to make a splash. ~ SPA
D7. An Egyptian goddess that looks like an old cow. ~ HATHOR
D8. These have caused a lot of suffering this year. ~ ACHES
D9. Smoking makes this one more beautiful. ~ MEERSCHAUM
D10. Because dad did this to the bills, he also did it to the office. ~ FOOTED
D11. What each and every Barrymore gobbles up. ~ ROLE
D13. What the Notre Dame team did on high. ~ SCORED
D14. This is always shrinking. ~ SHY
D15. The kind of chickens that taste good to a darkey. ~ STOLEN
D16. The first word in ambulance chasing. ~ SUE
D19. What he did to the man who gave him a job. ~ BLESSED
D20. Oh, boy, but this is great ! ~ BIG
D21. A good time to cultivate Mother Earth, and she may loosen up and help the unemployed situation. ~ SPRINGTIME
D23. A powerful wet influence. ~ SEA
D24. A well red man, and if you are well read you will know he belongs to the Algonquin tribe. ~ SAC
D27. Guys. ~ RIDICULES
D28. That part of the government machinery that needs fixing because of a few loose nuts. ~ SENATE
D30. Friends of the puzzle audience, this is may. ~ CAN
D31. An uncle that the antis want to boss. ~ SAM
D32. Just because Knute Rockne makes a good coach, doesn't mean that this one will ever make one (plur.). ~ SEDANS
D34. Where many great men were born. ~ HUT
D36. Good selling points. ~ MERITS
D37. This one will dance to any tune and with any partner if he is paid enough. ~ GIGOLO
D38. There is no virtue in this. ~ SIN
D41. Far from the madding crowd. ~ RURAL
D42. A strain from a stein song. ~ HIC
D44. The only convenience that is left out of kitchenette apartments. ~ AREA
D46. Because this has gone down it is going to be pretty soft for the motorists. ~ TIRE
D47. This is a big thing for the merchandise of Chicago. ~ MART
D49. There is a pile in this. ~ NAP
D50. This proved he was German. ~ VON
D52. The question is whether this is to- or not to-. ~ BE


